module gunbc.rung_drop.crate_row_names_a_module_the_emitter_has_no_reason_to_write

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.algebra { FreeSemigroup }
import gunbc.rung_drop { RungDrop, Standing, TypedDeclaration, Enumerated, ReplacementStaged }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, MechanicallyPreventable }

// THE DECLARED RUNG DROP for the whole-population coverage certification of the emitted-edge admission
// over the modules a crate row can name but the emitter has no reason to write. Authored 2026-11-25 by
// vivid-swift-693 (lane manager) on neat-badger-614's ruling, after the operator escalation msg_f631425e
// closed HOLD BY DEFAULT -- a timeout on the ask, so no operator words exist to record and no
// gunbc.design_ruling entry is claimed by this row.
//
// WHAT THE DROP COVERS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. The emitted-edge admission still runs, still refuses, and
// still names every uncovered endpoint: a round over --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 prints its
// Stage0EmittedEdgesNotCovered population and per-module counts, and a worker reads that count as the
// measure of their own placement. WHAT IS DECIDED NO FURTHER IS THE CONVERGENCE HALF: that a heal or
// regen round reaches the seed's fixed point once a crate row names a module with no emitted body. For
// those modules the round's own build refuses and the refusal is not the diff's defect, so the reading
// that used to be a blocking verdict about the change is now reported as a property of the partition.
// Nothing else loses its blocking result: a semantic red, a missing witness, a mirror drift, an
// unsealed candidate and a wall interruption all stay armed, on other gates.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT A STEP ORDERING A WORKER CAN REORDER, WHICH IS WHAT IT FIRST LOOKED LIKE. Measured
// 2026-11-25 on origin/main from the host realization of v2.workflow.required_regen, in the file
// src/v1/stage0/src/required_regen_host.rs: the round calls emit_generated_surface first, then
// write_emitted_tree, and only then reaches seed_cargo_build at its round.rebuild_from_installed sites.
// Emit, then write, then build. So a body absent at the build is not a body whose write step came too
// late; it is a body the emitter was never given a reason to produce, which the same file's own header
// comment distinguishes ("WHICH EMITTED SURFACES ARE MISSING BECAUSE THE CHANGE IS NEW, not because the
// emitter ..."). Reachability, not sequence, is the missing input: the fourteen claim-test module bodies
// that do exist in the seed root are each referenced by an authored source, and the modules listed below
// are referenced by nothing.
//
// MEASURED BASIS, all counts printed on origin/main the same day, no slot spent. Every module named by a
// GeneratedPartitionCrateRow has an emitted body on disk in src/v1/stage0/src: 100 named, 100 present, 0
// missing. The thirteen modules enumerated below have 0 bodies between them. Zero claim-test modules
// appear in the generated carrier or in src/v2/workflow/rust_crate_partition.dag, while fourteen
// claim-test bodies exist in the seed root and are named by src/v1/stage0/src/lib.rs with its 90 pub mod
// lines -- which is the host shell the admission's own refusal text names as the alternative to a
// partition row, in gunbc_stage0_stage0_emitted_edge_admission at its Stage0EmittedEdgesNotCovered
// display. A crate row naming a body-less module therefore makes the generated crate lib.rs declare a
// path to a file that does not exist, and the build behind it fails at cargo exit status 101 with
// "couldn't read ... No such file or directory", as observed in heal run 45582811039.
data crate_row_names_a_module_the_emitter_has_no_reason_to_write: RungDrop = RungDrop {
  identity: "crate_row_names_a_module_the_emitter_has_no_reason_to_write" as NonEmptyStr,

  subject: "the convergence half of the emitted-edge coverage certification over the modules a crate row names that no authored source references, and only that: the admission count over the declared partition is still read and still reported per module",

  declared: "2026-11-25",

  standing: Standing { declaration: TypedDeclaration {
    previous: MechanicallyPreventable,
    temporary: Mitigatable,
    reason: ReplacementStaged { replacement: "a placement route that gives the emitter a reason for each named module -- an authored reference for a module meant to be reachable, or host-shell placement named in the seed's own lib.rs population for one meant to be reachable only as a claim -- so no GeneratedPartitionCrateRow can name a module whose emitted body cannot exist" },
    population: Enumerated { members: FreeSemigroup { head: "extdeps_exec_command", tail: ["extdeps_tools_ls", "extdeps_posix_shell_command_language", "extdeps_filesystem_filesystem_io", "extdeps_tools_env", "extdeps_exec_program", "extdeps_posix_process", "gunbc_remote_shell_command", "extdeps_access_posix", "std_operation_argv", "std_template", "std_env_binding", "dry_run"] } },
    restoration_trigger: "AN OPERATOR RULING ON SEED GROWTH, recorded in gunbc.design_ruling with the operator's own words, that names which of these thirteen may have an emitted body and by which authored reference, and places the rest: the seed-input modules that belong in a crate row reach it through an authored reference so the emit writes the body, and any module meant to be reachable only as a claim is placed in the host shell instead of a crate row. This row then retires with its population verbatim. Two things do NOT retire it: rerunning the round unchanged, which reproduces the same cargo exit status 101 at the build behind the emit; and hand-writing a body, which is seed Rust and is outside every worker's authority on this lane. A default closure of the ask is not the ruling -- msg_f631425e closed Hold by timeout and is cited here precisely because it decided nothing.",
  } }
}
